1403:
** δοχ-ή**, ἡ,
={δοχεῖον}, **receptacle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 828, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 71c.}}
**II** **reception, entertainment,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.348f, LXX.Gen.21.8, +others, “PTeb.” 112.89 (2nd c.BC), NT.Luke.5.29, etc.}}; ={ἄριστον}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**III** σημεῖον ἐν θυτικῇ, {{Refs|[prev. author]}}

1404:
** δράκων** [ᾰ], οντος,
o(: (probably from δέρκομαι, δρᾰκεῖν,{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 3.8)}}:— **dragon, serpent,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.39, +others; interchangeable with ὄφις, 12.202, 208, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 322, 825, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.40, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 292 (Lyric poetry); ἀετὸς καὶ δ. πολέμια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 609a4}}; perhaps a **water-snake,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 602b25.}}
**II** the constellation **Draco,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 46, +others, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.69.}}
**III** a sea-fish, **the great weever,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 60, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 598a11, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.48.}}
**IV** ={κηρύκειον}, probably **a wand with a serpent coiled round** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 700 (compare 701).}}
**IV.2** **serpent-shaped bracelet** or **necklace,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 41.}}
**IV.3** a **noose** or **crossed bandage** for the ankle, Heraclas cited in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.5.1.}}
**IV.4** **dragon-standard,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 1.144, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 18.219a, compare “Or.” 1.2a}}: hence, **corps of** 1,000 **men** in the Parthian army, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist. Conscr.” 29.}}

1405:
** δράσσομαι**,
_Attic dialect_ **δράττομαι**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13}}; _imperfect_ ἐδραττόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 545}}: _future_ δράξομαι {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|“APl.” 4.275.10 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus), LXX.Num.5.26}}: _aorist_ ἐδραξάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 209e, etc.:}}_perfect_ δέδραγμαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2 _person_ δέδραξαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 750}}, _participle_ δεδραγμένος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.393:}} —the _active_, δράσσω only in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.155, “Etymologicum Magnum” 285.43, probably in “PLond.” 3.1170v113 (3rd c.AD), compare δράξαι· κρατῆσαι, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus: (compare δράξ, δράγμα, δραχμή)}}:—**grasp** with the hand, with _genitive_ _of things_, κόνιος δεδραγμένος αἱματοέσσης **clutching handfuls** of gory dust, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place]: _metaphorically_, ἐλπίδος δεδραγμένος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 235 (variants πεπρ-, πεφρ-),compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 36.15.7}}; δραξάμενοι τῶν ἁλῶν **taking a handful** of salt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus [same place], etc.}}
**2** **lay hold of,** τί μου δέδραξαι χερσ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 750}}; δραξάμενος φάρυγος **having seized** [them] by the throat, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.28, compare 25.145, “POxy.” 1298.10 (4th c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_, δράξασθαι καιροῦ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.67}}; μείζονος οἴκου (i.e. by marriage), {{Refs 6th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 1.14; μεγάλης ἀπήνης “Anthologia Graeca” 11.238 (6th c.BC: Demodocus Lyricus); τᾶς κραδίας 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 30.9}}; [ὧν χρ]ὴ δράξασθαι τὸ στόμα sounds the mouth has to **grip,** i.e. **make,** uncertain in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.41.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **take by handfuls,** ταύτας [τὰς μνέας] δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13}}; also, **catch,** τοὺς σοφοὺς ἐν τῇ πανουργίᾳ αὐτῶν {{NT|NT.1Cor.3.20.}}

1406:
** δραχμ-ή**, ἡ,
(δράσσομαι, properly **as much as one can hold in the hand,**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 17):}}
**I** a weight, **drachm,** [κρεῶν] probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.4, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 17, etc.}}
**I.2** a silver coin, **drachma,** worth six obols, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.144, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.18, “IG” 7.3171.52 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_), 9(1).694.54 (Corc.)}}, etc. [The _penultimate_ is long in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 157, and sometimes in Comedy texts, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 691 (_anapaest meter_), “Pax” 1201, “Pl.” 1019, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 174.17}}: **δαρχμή** is found in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; compare δαρκνά.]

1407:
** δρέπᾰνον**, τό,
also **δράπανον** (which see), (δρέπω) ={δρεπάνη}, δ. εὐκαμπές {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.368; χαλκέοις ἤμα δ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 534}}; the usual form in Prose and Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.125, etc.; δ. θεριστικόν “PMagd.” 8.6 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **pruning-knife,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 333d.}}
**3** **scythe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.30.}}
**4** **curved sword, scimitar,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.112, 7.93, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 576.}}

1408:
** δρόμος**, ὁ,
(δραμεῖν) **course, race,** in Il.mostly of horses, ἵπποισι τάθη δρόμος {{Refs|23.375; also of men, τέτατο δρόμος [prev. work] 758}}; οὐρίῳ δρόμῳ with prosperous **course,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 889 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἅπαντι χρῆσθαι τῷ δρόμῳ at full **speed,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dom.” 10}}: of **any quick movement,** e. g. **flight,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 207: of Time, ἡμέρης δ.}} a day's **running,** i. e. the distance one can go in a day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.5; κατανύσαι τὸν προκείμενον δ. [prev. author] 8.98; ἵππου δ. ἡμέρας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.273: of Things, δ. νεφέλης, ἡλίου τε καὶ σελήνης, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 163, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 370b (_plural_), etc.; οἱ δ. τῶν ἀστέρων 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Paraphrasis Ptolemaei Tetrabiblou” 136}}; δρόμῳ **at a run,** frequently with Verbs of motion, δρόμῳ διαβάντας τὸν Ἀσωπόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.59; ἰέναι [prev. author] 3.77; χρῆσθαι [prev. author] 6.112; χωρεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.31; δ. ξυνῆψαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1101; βοηθῆσαι δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 551: in _plural_, δρόμοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 838, “Supp.” 819.}}
**2** **foot-race,** as a contest, {{Refs|“IG” 2.594.11, +others: proverbial, περὶ τοῦ παντὸς δρόμον (-μου codices) θεῖν}} to **run** for one's life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.74; τὸν περὶ ψυχῆς δρόμον δραμεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 375; περὶ ψυχῆς ὁ δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173a}}: generally, **contest,** πλαγᾶν δρόμος, i. e. a pugilistic **contest,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).60.}}
**3** **lap** in a race, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 726 (interpolated [prev. work] 691); ἐν τῷ δευτέρῳ δ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 579a8.}}
**4** in speaking, **rapid delivery,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor “Ars Rhetorica” p.312S.}}
**II** **place for running,** δρόμοι εὐρέες **runs** for cattle, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.605.}}
**II.2** **racecourse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.126, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 599.}}
**II.3** **public walk,** ἐν εὐσκίοις δ. Ἀκαδήμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 32, compare “IG” 22.1126.36, etc.}}; **colonnade,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 144c; κατάστεγος δ.}}**cloister,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 273a; δ. ξυστός 5th c.BC: Aristias Tragicus 5}}; in Crete, ={γυμνάσιον}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, compare “SIG” 463.14 (Itanos, 3rd c.BC)}}; δύ᾽ ἢ τρεῖς δρόμους περιεληλυθότε having taken two or three **turns in the cloister,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthd.”}}[same place]; in Egypt, **avenue** of Sphinxes at entrance of temples, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 56.52 (Canopus, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus III), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.28, etc.; δ. τοῦ ἱεροῦ “BGU” 1130.10 (1st c.BC).}}
**II.4** **orchestra** in the theatre (_Tarentum dialect_), {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.5** _metaphorically_, ἔξω δρόμου or ἐκτὸς δρόμου φέρεσθαι get off the **course,** i. e. wander from the point, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 883 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 414b; ἐκ δρόμου πεσεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1245}}; οὐδέν ἐστ᾽ ἔξω δρόμου 'tis not foreign to the purpose, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 514.}}
**III** δ. δημόσιος, = Latin _cursus publicus,_ {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Vandalico” 1.16, “Arc.” 30, 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 2.10}}; δ. ὀξύς, = Latin _cursus velox,_ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. work]3.61, “POxy.” 900.7 (4th c.AD), etc.}}

1410:
** δύνᾰμαι** [ῠ],
_2nd pers._ _singular_ δύνασαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.393, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.374, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1164 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 811 (Lyric poetry), “Pl.” 574, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.8, etc.; δύνῃ “Carmen Aureum” 19, also in codices of 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 798, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 253, “Andr.” 239, and later Prose, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.11.5, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 13.32}}; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ δύνᾳ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15 ii 16, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.2, also 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 849 (Lyric poetry), uncertain in “OT” 696 (Lyric poetry)}}; δύνῃ is _subjunctive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 491, compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 337}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ δυνέαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.142}}; _subjunctive_ δύνωμαι, _Ionic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ δύνηαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.229 (δυνεώμεθα -ωνται as variants in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.97, 7.163); also δύνᾱμαι 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 3.3, “GDI” 4952 A 42 (from Crete)}}: _imperfect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἐδύνω [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 405, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.7; later ἐδύνασο 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 16 (variant{ἠδ}.), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 9.1}}]; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐδυνέατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.110, +others (ἠδ- codices)}}: _future_ δυνήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.238, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ δυνᾱσοῦμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 3; later δυνηθήσομαι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 52.37}}: _aorist_ ἐδυνησάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.33}}, _Epic dialect_ δυν- {{Refs|5.621}}; _subjunctive_ δυνήσωνται {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 1.17}}, never in good _Attic dialect_, _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.323:}}_passive_ forms, _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, Lyric poetry, ἐδυνάσθην or δυνάσθην [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.465, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.19, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.56, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 48 (variant{δυνηθείη}), also in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.24, “An.” 7.6.20}}]; Trag. and _Attic dialect_ Prose ἐδυνήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1067, “OT” 1212 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 867 (_anapaest meter_), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.80,186}}: _perfect_ δεδύνημαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.30, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.14, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.261S.}} —The double augment ἠδυνάμην is _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 175}}, but _Ionic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 2.374, and is found in codices of 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.110, +others, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26.β᾽, +others}}; ἠδύνω is required by metre in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 16; but is not found in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. before 4th c.BC, “IG” 22.678.12, +others, compare ἠδύνασθε [prev. work]7.2711 (Acraeph., 1st c.AD)}}; both forms occur in later writers: ἠδυνήθην occurs in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 208, and codices of 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.33, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.42, etc.: δύνομαι}} is a late form frequently in Papyrus as {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 9 (2nd c.BC), +others}} [ῠ, except in δῡναμένοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.276, 11.414, 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 15.1, and _proper name_ Δῡναμένη, for the sake of meter]}}
**I** **to be able, strong enough** to do, with _infinitive_ _present_ et _aorist_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.163, 1.562}}, etc.: _future_ _infinitive_ is _falsa lectio_ (πείσειν for πείθειν) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1394, (κωλύσειν for κωλῦσαι) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.11.13}}, etc.: frequently _absolutely_, with _infinitive_ supplied from the context, εἰ δύνασαί γε if at least thou **canst** (i.e. περισχέσθαι), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.393}}: also with _accusative_ _pronoun_ or _adjective_, ὅσσον δύναμαι χερσίν τε ποσίν τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|20.360; [Ζεὺς] δύναται ἅπαντα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.237}}; μέγα δυνάμενος very **powerful, mighty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.276, compare 11.414; δ. μέγιστον ξείνων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.9, etc.}}; μέγα δύναται, **multum valet**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 950 (Lyric poetry); δ. Διὸς ἄγχιστα [prev. author] “Supp.” 1035}}; οἱ δυνάμενοι **men of power, rank, and influence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 889, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.39, etc.}}; οἱ δυνάμενοι, opposed to οἱ μὴ ἔχοντες, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 255}}; opposed to οἱ πένητες, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 3}}; δυνάμενος παρά τινι **having influence** with him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.26, etc.; δύνασθαι ἐν τοῖς πρώτοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.105}}; δ. τοῖς χρήμασι, τῷ σώματι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.48, 24.4}}; ὁ δυνάμενος **one that can maintain himself**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 24.12}}; of things, [διαφέρει] οἷς δύνανται differ in their **potentialities**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.3.17.}}
**I.2** of moral possibility, **to be able, dare, bear** to do a thing, mostly with _negative_, οὔτε τελευτὴν ποιῆσαι δύναται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.250; σε.. οὐ δύναμαι προλιπεῖν 13.331, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 455; οὐκέτι ἐδύνατο ἐν τῷ καθεστῶτι τρόπῳ βιοτεύειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130; οὐδὲ σθένειν τοσοῦτον ᾠόμην τὰ σὰ κηρύγμαθ᾽ ὥστε.. θεῶν νόμιμα δύνασθαι.. ὑπερδραμεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 455.}}
**I.2.b** **enjoy a legal right**, δ. τῆς γεωργίας ἀπηλλάχθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 899.31 (2nd-3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**I.3** with ὡς and _superlative_, ὡς ἐδύναντο ἀδηλότατα as secretly as they **could**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.50}}; ὡς δύναμαι μάλιστα κατατείνας as forcibly as I **possibly can**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 367b; ὡς δύναιτο κάλλιστον [prev. author] “Smp.” 214c; ὡς ἂν δύνωμαι διὰ βραχυτάτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.3, etc.}}; simply ὡς ἐδύνατο in the best way he **could**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.2: with _relative_, ὅσους ἐδύνατο πλείστους ἀθροίσας [prev. author] “HG” 2.2.9; λαβεῖν.. οὓς ἂν σοφωτάτους δύνωμαι 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 213.}}
**II** **to be equivalent to**, λόγοι ἔργα δυνάμενοι words **that are as good as** deeds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.40: hence}}
**II.1** of money, **to be worth**, with _accusative_, ὁ σίγλος δύναται ἑπτὰ ὀβολούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.6, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.23}}: _absolutely_, **pass, be current**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Luct.” 10.}}
**II.2** of Number, etc., **to be equal** or **equivalent to**, τριηκόσιαι γενεαὶ δυνέαται μύρια ἔτεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.142}}; δυνήσεται τὴν ὑποτείνουσαν **will be equivalent to** the hypotenuse, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 709a19.}}
**II.3** of words, **signify, mean**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.110, +others; τὸ πειρηθῆναι καὶ τὸ ποιεῖν ἴσον δύναται [prev. author] 6.86. ; δύναται ἴσον τῷ δρᾶν τὸ νοεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 691; δύναται τὸ νεοδαμῶδες ἤδη ἐλεύθερον εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.58: in later Greek, δύναται τὸ μνασθέντι ἀντὶ τοῦ μνασθέντος}}**is equivalent to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.110.}}
**II.3.b** **avail to produce**, οὐδένα καιρὸν δύναται **brings** no advantage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 128 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 23d.}}
**II.3.c** of things, **mean, 'spell'**, τὸ τριβώνιον τί δύνατα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 842}}; αἱ ἀγγελίαι τοῦτο δύνανται they **mean** this much, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.36; τὴν αὐτὴν δ. δούλωσιν [prev. author] 1.141, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1313b25.}}
**II.4** in Mathematics texts, δύνασθαί τι **to be equivalent when squared to** a number or area, τοῖς ἐπιπέδοις ἃ δύνανται in the areas of which they [the lines] **are the roots**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 148b}}; ἡ ΒΓ τῆς Α μεῖζον δύναται τῇ ΔΖ **the square on** ΒΓ **is greater** than **the square on** A by the **square on** ΔΖ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 10.17}}; αἱ δυνάμεναι αὐτά [τὰ μεγέθη] the lines **representing** their **square roots**,{{Refs|[prev. work] “Def.” 4, compare “Prop.” 22}}; αὐξήσεις δυνάμεναί τε καὶ δυναστευόμεναι increments both **in the roots** and **powers** of numbers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 546b; τὴν ὑποτείνουσαν ταῖς περὶ τὴν ὀρθὴν ἴσον δυναμένην 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.720a, compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 17}}; ἡ δυναμένη, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for the **hypotenuse** of a right-angled triangle, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Metaphysica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 75.31.}}
**II.4.b** of numbers multiplied together, **come to**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 1.24, 27.}}
**III** _impersonal_, οὐ δύναται, with _aorist_ _infinitive_, **it cannot be, is not to be**, τοῖσι Σπαρτιήτῃσι καλλιερῆσαι οὐκ ἐδύνατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.134, compare 9.45}}; δύναται **it is possible**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.440e (assuming variant).}}

1411:
** δύνᾰμις** [ῠ], ἡ,
_genitive_ εως, _Ionic dialect_ ιος, _Ionic dialect_ _dative_ δυνάμι: (δύναμαι):— **power, might**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, especially of **bodily strength**, εἴ μοι δ. γε παρείη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.62, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.294; οἵη ἐμὴ δ. καὶ χεῖρες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.237; ἡ δ. τῶν νέων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.2, etc.}}: generally, **strength, power, ability** to do anything, πὰρ δύναμιν beyond one's **strength**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.787; in Prose, παρὰ δ. τολμηταί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70, etc.; ὑπὲρ δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.193}}; opposed to κατὰ δ. as far as **lies in one**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142, etc. (κὰδ δ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 336); εἰς δύναμιν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 172, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 458e, etc.; πρὸς τὴν δ. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 231a.}}
**2** **outward power, influence, authority**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 174 (_anapaest meter_), “Ag.” 779 (Lyric poetry); καταπαύσαντα τὴν Κύρου δ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90; δυνάμει προὔχοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, etc.}}; ἐν δ. εἶναι, γενέσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.5, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.29.}}
**3** **force for war, forces**, δ. ἀνδρῶν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.100, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 240d, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.41.2, LXX.Gen.21.22, “OGI” 139.8 (2nd c.BC)}}; μετὰ δυνάμεων ἱκανῶν Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 10 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; δ. καὶ πεζὴ καὶ ἱππικὴ καὶ ναυτική {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.12}}; πέντε δυνάμεσι πεφρουρημένον, of the five projecting rows of sarissae in the phalanx, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 5.2, +others}}
**4** **a power, quantity**, χρημάτων δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.ά}}.
**5** **means**, κατὰ δύναμιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1243b12}}; opposed to παρὰ δ., {{NT|NT.2Cor.8.3; κατὰ δ. τῶν ὑπαρχόντων “BGU” 1051.17 (Aug.).}}
**II** **power, faculty, capacity**, αἱ ἀμφὶ τὸ σῶμα δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 14; αἱ τοῦ σώματος δυνάμεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 185e; ἡ τῆς ὄψεως δ. [prev. author] “R.” 532a; ἡ τῶν λεγόντων δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.11}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, **capacity for**, τῶν ἔργων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a35; τοῦ λέγειν [prev. author] “Rh.” 1362b22}}; τοῦ λόγου, τῶν λόγων, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 578, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 94; δ. στρατηγική 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.84.6; δ. ἐν πραγματείᾳ [prev. author] 2.56.5; δ. συνθετική 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 2}}: _absolutely_, any **natural capacity** or **faculty**, that may be improved and may be used for good or ill, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 126a37, compare “MM” 1183b28.}}
**II.2** **elementary force**, such as heat, cold, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 16, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 646a14}}; ἡ τοῦ θερμοῦ δ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 650a5; θερμαντικὴ δ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 60, cf. Polystr. p.23 W.}}
**II.2.b** **property, quality**, ἰδίην δύναμιν καὶ φύσιν ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 13, compare “Nat.Hom.” 5, “Vict.” 1.10}}; especially of the **natural properties** of plants, etc., αἱ δ. τῶν φυομένων, τῶν σπερμάτων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.14, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.11.1}}; **productive power**, τῆς γῆς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 16.4; μετάλλων [prev. author] “Vect.” 4.1}}: generally, **function, faculty**, δύναμις φυσική, ζωική, ψυχική, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.635}}; περὶ φυσικῶν δ., title of work by Galen.
**II.2.c** in _plural_, **agencies**, ὑπάρχειν ἐν τῇ φύσει τὰς τοιαύτας δυνάμεις (i.e. the gods) Polystr.p.10 W.
**II.2.d** **function, meaning**, of part in whole, {{Refs|[prev. author] p.17 W.}}
**II.2.e** in Music, **function, value**, of a note in the scale, δ. ἐστι τάξις φθόγγου ἐν συστήματι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Cleonides Musicus “Introductio Harmonica - Musici Scriptores Graeci” 14, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.69M.}}; μέση κατὰ δύναμιν, opposed to κατὰ θέσιν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Harmonica - Opera Mathematica” 2.5.}}
**II.3** **faculty, art**, or **craft**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 532d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1018a30, “EN” 1094a10, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.1.1}}; δ. σκεπτική the **doctrine** of the Sceptics, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.1.}}
**II.4** **a medicine**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Timostratus Comicus 7, etc.; δ. ἁπλαῖ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 9, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.4, etc.; δ. πολυφάρμακοι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.403c, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.365}}: in _plural_, **collection of formulae** or **prescriptions**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.33.}}
**II.4.b** **action** of medicines, περὶ τῆς ἁπλῶν φαρμάκων δ., title of work by Galen; also, **potency**, δυνάμει θερμά, ψυχρά, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.672, +others}}
**II.5** **magically potent substance** or **object,** {{Refs|PMag.Leid.V. 8.12}}: in _plural_, **magical powers**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 4.7.}}
**III** **force** or **meaning** of a word, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 394b, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 12, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 31, etc.}}
**III.b** phonetic **value** of sounds or letters, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.47.8, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 12, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Jud.Voc.” 5, etc.}}
**III.2** **worth** or **value** of money, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, 2.97, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 9, “Sol.” 15.}}
**IV** **capability of existing** or **acting, potentiality**, opposed to **actuality** (ἐνέργεια), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1047b31, 1051a5, etc.: hence δυνάμει}} as _adverb_, **virtually**, ὕστερον ὂν τῇ τάξει, πρότερον τῇ δυνάμει.. ἐστί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.15}}; opposed to ἐνεργείᾳ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 86a28, +others}}; opposed to ἐντελεχείᾳ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 193b8, +others}}
**V** in Mathematics texts, **power**, κατὰ μεταφορὰν ἡ ἐν γεωμετρίᾳ λέγεται δ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1019b33}}; usually **second power, square**, κατὰ δύναμιν in **square**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 54b, compare “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 11, etc.: chiefly in _dative_, [εὐθεῖα] δυνάμει ἴση}} a line **the square** on which is equal to an area, ἡ BA ἐλάσσων ἐστὶν ἢ διπλασίων δυνάμει τῆς AK the **square** on BA is less than double of the **square** on AK, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.9: εὐθεῖαι δ. σύμμετροι}} commensurable **in square**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 10 “Def.” 2}}; ἡ δυνάμει δεκάς the series 12 + 22... + 102, {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 64.}}
**V.b** **square number**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 32a.}}
**V.c** **square** of an unknown quantity {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|(x2), 3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus “Def.” 2, +others}}
**V.2** **square root** of a number which is not a perfect square, **surd**, opposed to μῆκος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147d.}}
**V.3** **product** of two numbers, ἡ ἀμφοῖν (i.e. τριάδος καὶ δυάδος) δ. ἑξάς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.3, compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.108 P.}}; δυνάμει **in product**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Metrica” 1.15, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 33.}}
**VI** concrete, **powers**, especially of divine beings, αἱ δ. τῶν οὐρανῶν {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Isa.34.4, cf. NT.1Pet.3.22, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.587, “Corpus Hermeticum” 1.26, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.34: _sing._, NT.Act.8.10, “PMag.Par.” 1.1275}}; πολυώνυμος δ., of God, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 3.}}
**VII** **manifestation of divine power, miracle,** {{NT|NT.Matt.11.21, +others, Buresch “Aus Lydien” 113, etc.}}

1412:
** δῠνᾰμ-όω**,
**strengthen**, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Eccl.10.10, 2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX.Dan.9.27, 1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Callimachus” 30, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 35, Sall. 16, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 70, +others}}:—_passive_, {{NT|NT.Col.1.11, etc.}}
**2** in magic, **put power into**, σῶμα {{Refs|“PMag.Leid.W.” 7.16}}; πρᾶγμα{{Refs|[prev. work] “V.” 8.19; τινά “PMag.Par.” 1.197}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|“PMag.Berol.” 2.121.}}

1414:
** δῠνᾰτ-έω**,
={δύναμαι}, δυνατήσει τὸ συμβαῖνον ἴσχειν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 11.}}
**2** **to be mighty**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.13.3.}}

1415:
** δῠνᾰτ-ός**, ή, όν,
also ός, όν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.14, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Cit. 2}}:— **strong, mighty**, in body or mind, ὅ τι ἦν αὐτῶν δυνατώτατον **the ablest-bodied men**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.31}}; **sound in limb**, opposed to ἀδύνατος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.12; σῶμα δ. πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.23; χερσὶ καὶ ψυχᾷ δ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.39; τοῖς σώμασι καὶ ταῖς ψυχαῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.19; εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 366a; κατά τι [prev. work] 366d (_superlative_): with _accusative_, [prev. passage] (_superlative_)}}; of ships, **fit for service**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.60}}; of things, δυνατώτερον ἀδικία δικαιοσύνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 351a}}; λόγος a **powerful** argument, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.31U.; δ. προτείχισμα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.31.8.}}
**2** with _infinitive_, **able** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97, etc.}}; δ. λῦσαι **mighty** to loose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).9; λέγειν τε καὶ πράσσειν-ώτατος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319a}}; -ώτατοι καὶ τοῖς σώμασιν καὶ τοῖς χρήμασιν λῃτουργεῖν Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 29.5; ἐᾶν τοὺς δ. ἄρχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.3}}; ὅσονπερ δ. εἰμι, with _infinitive_ omitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 523.}}
**3** of outward power, **powerful, influential**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 219; τῶν Ἑλλήνων δυνατώτατοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.53}}; οἱ δ. **the chief men of rank and influence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65; χρήμασι δ. [prev. author] 1.13, etc., compare “OGI” 669.13 (1st c.AD).}}
**4** **able to produce, productive**, χώρα -ωτέρα εἰς τὴν καρπῶν ἔκφυσιν {{Refs|“Geoponica” 2.21.5.}}
**5** **potential**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1048a27.}}
**II** _passive_, of things, **possible**, οὐ δύνατον γένεσθαι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.21, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.111, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 97 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; ὁδὸς δυνατὴ καὶ τοῖς ὑποζυγίοις πορεύεσθαι **practicable**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.24; λόγου δ. κατανοῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 90c; βίον τοῖς πλείστοις κοινωνῆσαι δ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1295a30}}; κατὰ τὸ δυνατόν, **quantum fieri possit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 422d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.6, etc.; ἐς τὸ δ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.24; εἰς ὅσον ἀνθρώπῳ δ. μάλιστα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 277a; ἐκ τῶν δυνατῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.23; ἐπὶ τὸ δ. [prev. author] “Cyn.” 5.8; ἐν δυνατῷ εἶναι “BCH” 29.172 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; also ὅσον δυνατόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 997; ὅσον καθ᾽ ἡμᾶς δ. [prev. author] “Ba.” 183}}; especially with _superlative_, ὡς δ. πλεῖστον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.278; ὡς δ. κακίστους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.5; γνώμη ὡς δ. δικαιοτάτη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.13}}; τὰ δ. things **which are practicable**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.89, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1359b1.}}
**III** _adverb_ -τῶς **strongly, powerfully**, εἰπεῖν δ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.48}}: _superlative_ -ώτατα **most ably**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516d.}}
**III.2** δ. ἔχει it is **possible**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11.}}

1416:
** δύω**,
see at {δύο}.** δύω** (see. below), δῡνω:
**A** causal Tenses, **cause to sink, sink, plunge in**; _present_ only in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.4.8 οὐκ ἐν ἴσῳ βάθει πάντα δύοντες τῆς θαλάσσης:}}_aorist 1_ ἔδῡσα (ἐξ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.341}}; compare the compounds ἀπο-, ἐκ-, ἐν-, κατα-δύω.
**B** non-causal, **get** or **go into**, with _accusative_: _present_ δύω (see. {{Refs|1.4)}}; more frequently δύνω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.202, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 616, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1331, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ δῦνον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.268}}: _aorist_ ἔδῡνα {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 245}}, _participle_ δύνας {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.15.9, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.11.7, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 4.1, but ἔδῡσα NT.Mark.1.32, etc.}}: more frequently _middle_ δύομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.140, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 529 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc. (also in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., as {{Refs|“IG” 22.1241)}}: _imperfect_ ἐδυόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 269a}}; _Epic dialect_ δύοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.345:}}_future_ δύσομαι [ῡ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|] 7.298, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1271}}: _aorist_ ἐδῡσάμην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.865, (ἀπό) 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 302}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ δύσαντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.739, _optative_ δυσαίατο probably in 18.376 (Prose and Comedy texts in Compds.); 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus mostly uses the _Epic dialect_ forms ἐδύσεο, ἐδύσετο, _imperative_ δύσεο 19.36, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 108}}, _participle_ δυσόμενος (in _present_ sense) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.24, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 384}}: more frequently _aorist_ ἔδυν (as if from *δῦμι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.63, etc.; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἐδύτην [ῡ] 10.254}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἔδῡμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 367; ἔδῡτε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.106}}; ἔδῡσαν, _Epic dialect_ ἔδυν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.263}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ δύσκεν {{Refs|8.271; _imperative_ δῦθι, δῦτε, 16.64, 18.140}}_subjunctive_ δύω [ῡ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.340, 22.99, but δύῃ [ῠ] 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 728}}; _Epic dialect_ _optative_ δύη [ῡ] (for δυίη) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.348}}; _infinitive_ δῦναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.221, _Attic dialect_, _Epic dialect_ δύμεναι [ῡ] 14.63, ἐκ-δῦμεν 16.99}}; _participle_ δύς, δῦσα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.8}}: _perfect_ δέδῡκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.811, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116e}}; _Doric dialect_ _infinitive_ δεδυκεῖν [ῡ] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.102:}} —_passive_, _future_ and _aorist_ δῠθήσομαι, ἐδύθην [ῠ], and a _perfect_ δέδῠμαι only in compounds, see at {ἀπο-}, ἐκ-, ἐν-δύω. [ῠ in δύω in _present_ and _imperfect_ _active_ and _middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus; but 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus}} has δῡομαι, δῡετο {{Refs|1.581}}_participle_ δῡόμενος{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 925, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 22; δῡεται 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 7.286; ἐκ-δέδῠκας “Anthologia Graeca” 5.72 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus).]}}
**B.I** of Places or Countries, **enter, make one's way into**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus the most frequently use, εἰ.. κε πύλας καὶ τείχεα δύω (_aorist_ 2) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.99; πόλιν δύσεσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.18}}; ἔδυ νέφεα **plunged into** the clouds, of a star, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.63}}; δῦτε θαλάσσης εὐρέα κόλπον **plunge into** the lap of Ocean, {{Refs|18.140}}; γαῖαν ἐδύτην **went beneath** the earth, i.e. died, {{Refs|6.19, compare 411}}, etc.; πόλεμον δύμεναι **plunge into**..,{{Refs|14.63; θεῖον δύσονται ἀγῶνα 7.298; ἐδύσετο οὐλαμὸν ἀνδρῶν 20.379}}; δύσεο δὲ μνηστῆρας **go in to** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.276}}; rarely in Trag., αἰθέρα δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1192 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1271.}}
**B.I.2** in _Epic dialect_ less frequently with Preps., ἔδυν δόμον Ἄϊδος εἴσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.263; δύσομαι εἰς Ἀΐδαο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.383; ἐς πόντον ἐδύσετο 5.352; δέρτρον ἔσω δύνοντες 11.579; δύσεθ᾽ ἁλὸς κατὰ κῦμα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.136; ὑπὸ κῦμα θαλάσσης αὐτίκ᾽ ἔδυσαν 18.145}}; κατὰ σταθμοὺς δύεται **slinks** into the fold,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.140; καθ᾽ ὅμιλον ἔδυ Τρώων 3.36 (rarely with _genitive_, κατὰ σπείους κοίλοιο δέδυκεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.93)}}; πάϊς ὣς ὑπὸ μητέρα δύσκεν εἰς Αἴαντα **he got himself unto** Ajax, i.e. **got behind** his shield, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.271}}; βέλος δ᾽ εἰς ἐγκέφαλον δῦ{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 85; ἀκίδες δεδυκυῖαι διὰ φλεβῶν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 25}}; in Prose and Trag. mostly with a _preposition_ (but δυόμενοι _absolutely_, **diving**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25), δῦναι ἐς θάλασσαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.8; ἐς ἄντρον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 261; ἁρμὸν.. πρὸς αὐτὸ στόμιον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1217; κατὰ βάθος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 905a; κατὰ τῆς γῆς [prev. author] “Phd.” 113c, etc.}}
**B.I.3** _absolutely_, εἴσω ἔδυ ξίφος the sword **entered his body**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.340}}; δύνει ἀλοιφή **sinks in** (where however βοείην may be supplied), {{Refs|17.392}}:—_middle_, δύου πάλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 148.}}
**B.I.4** of Sun and Stars, **sink into** [the sea], **set**, ἠέλιος μὲν ἔδυ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.241, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.329, etc.; ἔδυ φάος ἠελίοιο 13.35; δύσετό τ᾽ ἠέλιος 2.388, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.465, etc.; ἀελίω δύντος 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 25.8}}; so Βοώτης ὀψὲ δύων late-**setting** Boötes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.272; δείελος ὀψὲ δύων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.232; [σελαναία] δύεν 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 8.6; πρὸ δύντος ἡλίου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.149; πρὸ ἡλίου δύντος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.22}}; δυσόμενος Υπερίων (to mark the West) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.24}}; ἐδύετο εἰς τόπον [ὁ ἥλιος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 269a}}; πρὸς δύνοντος ἡλίου towards the West, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 255: _metaphorically_, βίου δύντος αὐγαί [prev. author] “Ag.” 1123 (Lyric poetry); ἔδυ πρόπας δόμος [prev. work] 1011 (Lyric poetry)}}; δεδυκὸς ζῆν live **in retirement**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 781c.}}
**B.II** of clothes and armour, **get into**, Ἀρήϊα τεύχεα δ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.340, etc.}}; κυνέην δ. **put on** one's helmet,{{Refs|5.845; δῦ δὲ χιτῶν᾽ 18.416: _metaphorically_, εἰ μὴ σύ γε δύσεαι ἀλκήν}} if thou **wilt** not **put on** strength,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|9.231; so ἀνάγκας ἔδυ λέπαδνον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 218 (Lyric poetry): hence}}
**B.II.2** _transitive_, **put on**, ἀμφ᾽ ὤμοισιν ἐδύσετο τεύχεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.328, etc.; ὤμοιϊν.. τεύχεα δῦθι 16.64; χιτῶνα περὶ χροΐ.. δῦνεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.61; χρυσὸν.. ἔδυνε περὶ χροΐ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.43.}}
**B.II.3** rarely _absolutely_ with a _preposition_, ὅπλοισιν ἔνι δεινοῖσιν ἐδύτην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.272, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.638; ἐς τεύχεα δύντε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.201.}}
**B.III** of sufferings, passions, and the like, **enter, come over** or **upon**, κάματος.. γυῖα δέδυκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.811; ὄφρ᾽ ἔτι μᾶλλον δύη ἄχος κραδίην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.348; ἦτορ δῦν᾽ ἄχος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.367; ὀδύναι δῦνον μένος 11.272}}; κρατερὴ δέ ἑ λύσσα δέδυκε madness **is come over** him, {{Refs|9.239}}; δῦ μιν Ἄρης Ares, i.e. the spirit of war, **filled** him,{{Refs|17.210; μιν ἔδυ χόλος 19.16.}}

1422:
** δύσ-κολος**, ον,
(κόλον):
**I** of persons, properly **hard to satisfy with food** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.262a)}}: but, generally, **hard to please, discontented, fretful, peevish**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 942; γῆρας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1251; δ. ψυχὴ καὶ ἀγρία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 649e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1108a30, etc.; τὸ δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 791c}}; of animals, **intractable**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 174d (_comparative_): so in}} _adverb_ δυσκόλως, ἔχειν, διακεῖσθαι πρός τινα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.132, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.33; δυσκολώτερον διακεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84e.}}
**II** of things, **troublesome, harassing**, δ. ἡ ἡνιόχησις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 246b; πυρετοί 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 38}}: generally, **unpleasant**, ἄν τι δ. συμβῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.189, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 89; εἴ τι δ. πέπρακται Θηβαίοις πρὸς ἡμᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.176}}; καιροὶ δ. **difficult** times, {{Refs|“IG” 22.682.33}}. _adverb_ -λως, ὑπακούειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.8.}}
**II.2** **difficult to explain**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 180b5, “Metaphorical” 1001b1}}; δ. ἐστι it is **difficult,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Mark.10.24, compare 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 1.15 (_comparative_)}}; τὰ μὲν ῥάδια.. τὰ δὲ δ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 994.24}}. _adverb_ -λως **hardly, with difficulty,** {{NT|NT.Mark.10.23, +others}}

