1424:
** δυσμή**, ἡ,
(δύω) ={δύσις}, **setting**, mostly in _plural_, ἀελίου δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1245, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 69, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61e}}; ἐπὶ δυσμῇσιν ἐών at the point of **setting**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104; περὶ ἡλίου δυσμάς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.39; ἥλιος ἦν ἤδη περὶ δυσμάς “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 15.5: _metaphorically_, τὸ γῆρας δυσμαὶ βίου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1457b25, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.79, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.678, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.90, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 2, etc.}}
**II** **the quarter of sunset, west**, ἀπὸ ἑσπέρης τε καὶ [ἡλίου] δυσμέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.31; πρὸς ἡλίου δυσμέων [prev. author] 7.115, compare 2.33; πρὸς δυσμαῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 232}}; opposed to ἀνατολαί, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“BGU” 1049.8 (4th c.AD)}}:—also **δυθμή**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 10 (_plural_), “fragment” 539 (_sing._).}}

1425:
** δυσ-νόητος**, ον,
**hard to be understood**, Dariusap. {{NT+2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.13, NT.2Pet.3.16; χρησμοί 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 54.}}
**II** _active_, **slow of understanding**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 345.26.}}

1426:
** δυσφημ-ία**, ἡ,
**ill language**, especially **words of ill omen**, κατεῖχε.. πᾶν στρατόπεδον δυσφημίαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 10, uncertain in 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 16.4.1}}: _plural_, **curses**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.587f, compare “Pel.” 8}}; but, **unsavoury details**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 302.}}
**II** **blasphemy, slander**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.48, etc.}}
**III** **ill fame, obloquy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 178 (_plural_), 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 7.99c.}}

1428:
** δωδέκᾰτος**, η, ον,
**twelfth**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.781, etc.; δ. τόκοι, 8 1/3%, “SIG” 364.74 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc.:—_Epic dialect_ **δυωδ-**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.493, etc.}}
**II** δωδεκάτη, ἡ, ={Χόες}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1430:
** δῶμα**, ατος, τό,
(δέμω) **house**, πατρώϊον ἵκετο δ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.44, etc.}}; mainly _poetry_, but once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 2.62 (_plural_), and in late Prose (see. below)}}, but never in _Attic dialect_ Prose: also, **chief room, hall**, θάλαμον καὶ δ. καὶ αὐλήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.316, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.329, +others}}: hence, _plural_ for **a single house**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.259, frequently in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 607, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 332, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 301, etc.}}
**2** of the gods, ἀθάνατοι Ὀλύμπια δώματ᾽ ἔχοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.13, etc.; κλυτὰ δ. βένθεσι λίμνης, of Poseidon, 13.21}}; frequently of Pluto, δῶμ᾽ Ἀΐδαο the nether world, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.21; ὦ δῶμ᾽ Ἀΐδου καὶ Περσεφόνης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 110; Πλούτωνος δ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 808 (Lyric poetry)}}; of a temple, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.53, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 242, etc.: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.62, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 71.}}
**3** δῶμα Καδμεῖον, i.e. Thebes, {{Refs|[prev. work] 29.}}
**4** **housetop**, {{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Deu.22.8, NT.Matt.24.17, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 5.5, “POxy.” 475.22 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **household, family**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1468 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1226, etc.}}; compare δῶ.

1431:
** δωρ-εά**,
_Ionic dialect_ **δωρ-εή**, ἡ: δωρειά in earlier Attic Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 12.77, +others, δωρεά first in [prev. work] 22.1.68}}:— **gift, present**, especially **bounty** (={δόσις ἀναπόδοτος} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 125a18), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.140; δωρεὰν διδόναι [prev. author] 6.130, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 340}}; πορεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 616; δωρεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 290c}}; δ. δέχεσθαι, λαμβάνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.31, 15.40; ironically, θάνατόν τινι δωρεὰν ἀποδοῦναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.34; δ. ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1032, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.312; ἐν χάριτος μέρει καὶ δωρειᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.165}}; δωρειὰν καὶ χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]172,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844d}}; of a **legacy**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.41, 65}}; δωρεαί **privileges** and **immunities**, opposed to δῶρα, **gifts in cash** or **kind**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.10.4.}}
**2** **estate granted** by a king, **fief**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phoenicides Comicus 4.7, “PSI” 5.511.4, 518.2 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** _accusative_ δωρεάν as _adverb_, **as a free gift, freely**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.23, probably in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.4; μηδὲν δ. πράττειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.34.7, cf. LXX.Job.1.9; δ. λειτουργεῖν “Test.Epict.” 4.27, compare “Inscription Prien.” 4.17 (4th c.BC)}}; so κατὰ δωρεάν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 7.2711.13, +others (Acraeph., 1st c.AD)}}; ἐν δωρεᾷ προσνεῖμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.5.4}}; but γῆν (ἀμπελῶνα, etc.) ἐν δωρεᾷ ἔχειν to hold land by a royal **grant, PRev. Laws** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|36.15 (3rd c.BC), compare 43.11, 44.3.}}
**II.2** **to no purpose, for naught,** {{NT|NT.Gal.2.21.}}

1432:
Included with: ** δωρ-εά**,
_Ionic dialect_ **δωρ-εή**, ἡ: δωρειά in earlier Attic Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 12.77, +others, δωρεά first in [prev. work] 22.1.68}}:— **gift, present**, especially **bounty** (={δόσις ἀναπόδοτος} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 125a18), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.140; δωρεὰν διδόναι [prev. author] 6.130, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 340}}; πορεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 616; δωρεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 290c}}; δ. δέχεσθαι, λαμβάνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.31, 15.40; ironically, θάνατόν τινι δωρεὰν ἀποδοῦναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.34; δ. ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1032, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.312; ἐν χάριτος μέρει καὶ δωρειᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.165}}; δωρειὰν καὶ χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]172,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844d}}; of a **legacy**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.41, 65}}; δωρεαί **privileges** and **immunities**, opposed to δῶρα, **gifts in cash** or **kind**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.10.4.}}
**2** **estate granted** by a king, **fief**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phoenicides Comicus 4.7, “PSI” 5.511.4, 518.2 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** _accusative_ δωρεάν as _adverb_, **as a free gift, freely**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.23, probably in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.4; μηδὲν δ. πράττειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.34.7, cf. LXX.Job.1.9; δ. λειτουργεῖν “Test.Epict.” 4.27, compare “Inscription Prien.” 4.17 (4th c.BC)}}; so κατὰ δωρεάν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 7.2711.13, +others (Acraeph., 1st c.AD)}}; ἐν δωρεᾷ προσνεῖμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.5.4}}; but γῆν (ἀμπελῶνα, etc.) ἐν δωρεᾷ ἔχειν to hold land by a royal **grant, PRev. Laws** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|36.15 (3rd c.BC), compare 43.11, 44.3.}}
**II.2** **to no purpose, for naught,** {{NT|NT.Gal.2.21.}}

1433:
** δωρ-έω**,
_future_ -ήσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “fragment” 17}}: _aorist_ ἐδώρησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 82, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.78}}:—**give, present**, δῶρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}} [same place]; **present** one **with**, θυσίαις Ἑρμᾶν Pi.[same place]:—_passive_, _aorist_ δωρηθῆναι **to be given** or **presented**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.26}}: in _perfect_ _passive_, παρὰ θεῶν δῶρα ὑμῖν δεδώρηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 274c}}; and of persons, **to be presented with** a thing, χώρῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.85, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1029.}}
**II** more frequently _middle_ **δωρέομαι**, ῥεῖα θεὸς.. ἵππους δωρήσαιτ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.557}}; δωρέεσθαί τί τινι **present** a thing **to** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.126, 5.37, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 253, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.20, etc.; σπέρμα εἰς Πελοπόννησον δωρήσασθε [prev. author] “HG” 6.3.6}}; also δ. τινά τινι **present** one **with** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.54, 3.130, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 778}}; δ. τινά **to make** him **presents**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.55}}: _perfect_, δεδώρηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 46e, “Lg.” 672b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” [5.2.8].}}
**II.2** in _present_ and _imperfect_ also, **offer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 875.}}

1435:
** δῶρον**, τό,
(δίδωμι) **gift, present, gift of honour**, ἀγλαὰ δ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.213, etc.}}; **votive gift** or **offering** to a god, φέρε δῶρον Ἀθήνῃ {{LXX+NT+4th c.BC+|6.293, cf. LXX.Gen.4.4, NT.Mark.7.11; βωμοὶ δώροισι φλέγονται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 91}}; ποῦ μοι τὰ.. δ. κἀκροθίνι; {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 184}}; δῶρά τινος **the gifts of**, i. e. **given by**, him, θεῶν ἐρικυδέα δ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.265, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.142}}; δῶρ᾽ Ἀφροδίτης, i.e. personal charms, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.54, 64; δ. Κύπριδος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 363 (Lyric poetry)}}; δ. τῶν Μουσῶν καὶ Ἀπόλλωνος, of μουσική, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 796e:}} with _genitive_ _of things_, ὕπνου δ. **the blessing** of sleep, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.482}}; δῶρα **presents** given as tribute, {{Refs|17.225}}; δῶρον τοῦ ποταμοῦ, of the land of Egypt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.5.}}
**2** δῶρα **presents**, as retaining fees or bribes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.109, Jusj.[prev. work] 24.150, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 55.5, “SIG” 953.7 (from Calymna)}}, etc. (the usual sense of the word in _Attic dialect_ Oratt.): hence in _Attic dialect_ law, δώρων γραφή an indictment **for being bribed**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.232, etc., compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus}}; δώρων κριθῆναι to be tried **for taking bribes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.3}}; δώρων ἑλεῖν τινα to convict him **of taking bribes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 591}}; δ. ὀφλεῖν to be found guilty **of taking bribes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.74; δώρων δίωξις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 32.}}
**3** in _plural_, **good qualities, talents**, τὰ βασιλέως δ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 19.}}
**II** **front part of palm**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.144.}}
**II.2** **hand's breadth, palm**, as a measure of length, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 398, 1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 2.3.3, “Milet.” 7.57 (from Didyma)}}; compare δεκάδωρος.

1437:
** ἐάν**
(so early Attic Inscrr., as {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.3.20, εἰάν sometimes after BC 400 4th c.BC, [prev. work]22.28.17,compare “PEleph.” 1.8, 10 (4th c.BC))}}, also _contraction_ **ἤν** and **ἄν**, see at {ἤν},{ἄν} (B) [ᾱ], which by crasis with καί become κἄν:—**if haply, if**, regularly followed by _subjunctive_: for its use and for examples, see at {εἰ} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11, and ἄν (A) 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.1.}}
**II** in Hellenistic and late Greek, ={ἄν} after relative Pronouns and Conjunctions, as ὃς ἐάν who**soever**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.15.14, “PTeb.” 107.8 (2nd c.BC), NT.Matt.5.19, +others; ὅσος ἐάν “PPetr.” 3p.120 (3rd c.BC), NT.Matt.18.18; ὅστις ἐάν 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.220, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 9.23; ὅπου ἐάν NT.Matt.8.19, etc.; ὅθεν ἐάν “Geoponica” 1.3.3: followed by _indicative_, LXX.1Ki.2.14. [The second syllable of ἐάν is long, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1407, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 228, 4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 6.9.]}}

1438:
** ἑαυτοῦ**, ῆς, οῦ, ἑαυτῷ, ῇ, ῷ, ἑαυτόν, ήν, ό,
_plural_ ἑαυτῶν, ἑαυτοῖς, ἑαυτούς άς, ά: _Ionic dialect_ **ἑωυτοῦ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 57.44 (Milet., 5th c.BC), etc.}}; also ωὑτῆς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.84, ωὑτέου 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.7 (_Ionic dialect_ ἑωυ- by contraction of ἕο αὐ-, from which also _Attic dialect_ ἑᾱυ-, frequently written ἑατοῦ}} in Papyrus and Inscrr., as {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“SIG” 774.2 (Delph., 1st c.BC)}}: _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ **αὑτοῦ**, etc., which is the usual form in Trag., though ἑαυτοῦ, etc., are used (though rarely) when the metre requires, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 188 (_anapaest meter_), +others; in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. αὑτοῦ prevails after BC 300 4th c.BC}}; Cretan dialect **ϝιαυτοῦ** Kohler-Ziebarth **Stadtrecht von Gortyn** {{Refs|p.34}}; _Doric dialect_ **αὐταυτοῦ**, **αὐσαυτοῦ** (which see); Thess. **εὑτοῦ** (_dative_), {{Refs|“IG” 9(2).517.16}}: _genitive_ _plural_ **ηὑτῶν** {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 251 A 44 (from Cos)}}:—_reflexive_ _pronoun_ of _3rd pers._, **of himself, herself, itself**, etc.; first in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 78, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, and _Attic dialect_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ἕο αὐτοῦ, οἷ αὐτῷ, ἑ αὐτόν): αὐτὸ ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτό (variant -τοῦ)}} itself by **itself, absolutely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152b}}; αὐτὸ ἐφ᾽ αὑτοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 160b; ὅταν τὸ ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτὸν ἕκαστος σπεύδῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141; αὐτὸ καθ᾽ αὑτό 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157a; αὐτὰ πρὸς αὑτά [prev. work] 154e}}; ἀφ᾽ ἑαυτῶν, ἑαυτοῦ, of **themselves, himself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.60, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.10.3}}; ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ, see at {ἐπ; ἐν ἑαυτῷ γίγνεσθαι}, ἐντὸς ἑαυτοῦ γ., see at {ἐν}, ἐντό; παρ᾽ ἑαυτῷ at **his own** house, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.13.3, etc.: especially with _comparative_ and _superlative_, ἐγένοντο ἀμείνονες αὐτοὶ ἑωυτῶν they surpassed}}**themselves**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.86}}; πλουσιώτεροι ἑαυτῶν continually richer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8; θαρραλεώτεροι αὐτοὶ ἑαυτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 350a}}, compare d; τῇ αὐτὸ ἑωυτοῦ ἐστι μακρότατον at its very greatest length, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.8, compare 149, 4.85, 198.}}
**II** in _Attic dialect_, Trag., and later, αὑτοῦ, etc., is used for the _1st pers._ or _2nd pers._, as for ἐμαυτοῦ, αὐτὸς καθ᾽ αὑτοῦ τἄρα μηχανορραφῶ [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 221, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 138, etc.; for σεαυτοῦ, μόρον τὸν αὑτῆς οἶσθα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1297, compare 1141, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101c (variant), 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 59.16, etc.: so in _plural_, τὰ αὑτῶν (={ἡμῶν αὐτῶν}) ἐκποριζώμεθα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82; δώσομεν ἑαυτούς 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 47}}]; ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτοῖς by **ourselves**, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.1Ki.14.9, compare “PPar.” 47.26 (2nd c.BC), NT.2Cor.7.1, etc.}}; ἑαυτῶν, ={ὑμῶν αὐτῶν}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 63.128 (2nd c.BC).}}
**III** _plural_, ἑαυτῶν, ἑαυτοῖς, etc., is sometimes used for ἀλλήλων, ἀλλήλοις, **one another**, διάφοροι ἑωυτοῖσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.49; παρακελευόμενοι ἐν ἑαυτοῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25, etc.}}; καθ᾽ αὑτοῖν **one** against **the other**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 144 (_anapaest meter_); πρὸς αὑτούς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.19; περιιόντες αὑτῶν πυνθάνονται [prev. author] 4.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 215b.}}

1439:
** ἐάω**,
_contraction_ **ἐῶ** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.428, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ **εἰῶ** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.55; _Epic dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐάᾳς, ἐάᾳ, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.137, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.414}}; _infinitive_ ἐάαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.509:}}_imperfect_ εἴων,-ας, -α, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.448, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.25, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.28, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἔων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.2, ἔα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.517, 16.731; also ἔασκον or εἴασκον, 2.832, 5.802}}, etc.: _future_ ἐάσω [ᾱ] {{Refs|18.296}}, etc.: _aorist_ εἴᾱσα (εἴᾰσεν is variant for{εἴᾱς}' in {{Refs|10.299) 24.684, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔᾱσα {{Refs|11.437}}: _perfect_ εἴᾱκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.37, 43.78, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 17.35:}} —_passive_, _future_ ἐάσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 331, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142}}: _aorist_ εἰάθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.97}}: _perfect_ _passive_ εἴᾱμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.22.— 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} never uses the augment in this Verb. [ᾰ in _present_ and _imperfect_, ᾱ in _future_ and _aorist_ even in _Ionic dialect_ (so probably in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 56, 57}}; forms with -ασς- occur as variants in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.7)}}. Synizesis occurs in _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐᾷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.256, in 1}} _subjunctive_ ἐῶμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.344, and probably in ἐάσουσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.233}}; also in Trag., in _imperative_ ἔα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1451, “Ant.” 95, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 932; _indicative_ ἐῶ [prev. author] “Lys.” 734: 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus has the form ἦσεν· εἴασεν, compare ἦσαι· παῦσαι]}}:—**suffer, permit**, with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, τούσδε δ᾽ ἔα φθινύθειν **leave** them **alone** to perish, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.346; αἴ κεν ἐᾷ με.. ζώειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.359, etc.; ἐᾶν οἰκεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.48, compare “IG” 12.1; ἐ. τοὺς Ἕλληνας αὐτονόμους [prev. work]2.17.9; ἐᾶν ἄκλαυτον, ἄταφον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 29, compare “Tr.” 1083; ἐᾶν τί τινι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.233d:}} —so in _passive_, Κρέοντί γε θρόνους ἐᾶσθαι **should be given up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 368.}}
**b** **concede, allow** in argument, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 135b.}}
**2** with _negative_, οὐκ ἐᾶν not **to suffer**: hence, **forbid, prevent**, τρεῖν μ᾽ οὐκ ἐᾷ Παλλὰς Ἀθήνη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.256; εἴπερ γὰρ φθονέω τε καὶ οὐκ εἰῶ διαπέρσαι 4.55}}; especially of the law, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.21; δμωὰς δ᾽ οὐκ εἴας προβλωσκέμεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.25, etc.: used elliptically with ἀλλά}} following, οὐκ ἐῶν φεύγειν, ἀλλὰ [κελεύων] μένοντας ἐπικρατέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.21}}; also, **persuade** or **advise** not to do.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.133: an}}_infinitive_ may frequently be supplied, οὐκ ἐάσει σε τοῦτο will not **allow** thee [to do] this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 538}}; κἂν μηδεὶς ἐᾷ even if all men **forbid**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1184, compare “Ph.” 444:}} —so in _passive_, οὐκ ἐᾶσθαι, with _infinitive_, **to be hindered**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1344, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.16.}}
**II** **let alone, let be**, with _accusative_, ἔα χόλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.260}}; μνηστήρων μὲν ἔα βουλήν **heed not** the suitors' plan, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.281}}; ἐπεί με πρῶτον ἐάσας as soon as thou **hast dismissed** me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.557, compare 569, 684}}; ἤ κέν μιν ἐρύσσεαι, ἦ κεν ἐάσῃς or **wilt leave** him **alone**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|20.311, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108, etc.; ἐάσωμεν ἕκηλον αὐτόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 825; [πρᾶγμα] ἀκάθαρτον ἐᾶν [prev. author] “OT” 256; τὰ παθήματα.. παρεῖσ᾽ ἐάσω [prev. author] “OC” 363, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.36; ἐᾶν φιλοσοφίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 484c: with}}_infinitive_, ἐπὶ Σκύθας ἰέναι.. ἔασον **let** it **alone**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134}}; κλέψαι μὲν ἐάσομεν Ἕκτορα we **will have done with** stealing Hector, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.71; ἐᾶν περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 347c, etc.; ἐῶ γὰρ εἰ φίλον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.122: _absolutely_, ἀλλ᾽ ἄγε δὴ καὶ ἔασον}} **have done, let be**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.221, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 334; οὐ χρὴ μάχεσθαι πρὸς τὸ θεῖον, ἀλλ᾽ ἐᾶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 491.5}}; θεὸς τὸ μὲν δώσει, τὸ δ᾽ ἐάσει he will give one thing, the other he will **let alone**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.444}}:—_passive_, ἡ δ᾽ οὖν ἐάσθω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 329, etc.}}
**II.2** for ἐᾶν χαίρειν, see at {χαίρω} entry near the end (ἐϝάω, compare ἔβασον· ἔασον, and εὔα· ἔα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus who also has ἔησον· ἔασον.)}}

1442:
** ἕβδομος**, η, ον,
(ἑπτά) **seventh**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.117, etc.}}; ἡ ἑβδόμη **the seventh day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.57 (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 588a8}}; **sabbath**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.675, NT.Heb.4.4.}}
**2** ={ἑπτά}, ἑβδόμαις πύλαις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 125 (Lyric poetry, assuming variant), cf. Thom.Mag. p.133 R.; ἐν κύκλοισιν ἑβδόμοις “Milet.” 6.46.}}
**3** ἕβδομα, τά, **seven years' work**, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.29.27.}}

1448:
** ἐγγίζω**,
_aorist_ ἤγγισα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus (see. below)}}: _perfect_ ἤγγικα {{LXX+NT|LXX.Eze.7.4(7), NT.Matt.3.2}}: (ἐγγύς):—**bring near, bring up to**, τῇ γῇ τὰς ναῦς {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.4.7; τὰ φιλήματα τοῖς χείλεσι 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 2.37; τινὰ πρός τινα LXX.Gen.48.10.}}
**II** mostly _intransitive_, **approach**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 845a20; τινί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.4.1: with _genitive_, τῆς Αἰτωλίας [prev. author] 4.62.5, etc.; πρὸς τὸν θεόν LXX.Exo.19.21}}; εἰς θάνατον{{LXX|LXX.Job.33.22}}; ἕως{{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.37.30(33); μέχρι θανάτου NT.Phil.2.30}}; **to be imminent**, ἤγγικεν ἡ παρουσία τοῦ Κυρίου {{NT|NT.Jam.5.8}}: also, with _genitive_, **approximate to**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1457.4.}}
**II.2** **to be next of kin**, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.21.3.}}
**III** with _infinitive_, **to be on the point of** doing, ναοῦ -οντος συμπεσεῖν {{Refs|“IG” 12(1).1270.8 (from Syme).}}

1449:
** ἐγγρᾰφ-ω**,
**make incisions into**, τὸ στέλεχος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.1.2.}}
**2** **mark in or on, paint on**, ζῷα ἐς τὴν ἐσθῆτα ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.203}}; opposed to ἐξαλείφω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 501b.}}
**3** **engrave, inscribe**, ἐν τῇσι στήλῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.102, compare 4.91; νόμους 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.2 (of codifiers, opposed to ἐξαλείφω)}}: —_middle_, ἢν ἐγγράφου σὺ μνήμοσιν δέλτοις φρενῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 789}}:— _passive_, **to be written in**, ἐνεγέγραπτο δὲ τάδε ἐν αὐτῇ (i.e. τῇ ἐπιστολῇ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128}}; αὑτὸν εὗρεν ἐγγεγραμμένον κτείνειν found his name **entered in the letter** for execution,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 132; δέλτον ἐγγεγραμμένην συνθήμαθ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 157.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, εἰ μέλλουσι τοιαῦται διάνοιαι ἐγγραφήσεσθαι ἀνθρώποις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.52.}}
**5** Geometry texts, **inscribe** a figure in another, εἰς.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 4.4, +others}}; ἐν.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Sph. Cyl.” 1.13, +others (_passive_).}}
**6** in Medicine texts, **include in a prescription**, οἶνος ἐγγεγράφθω {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.2.}}
**II** **enter in the public register**, especially of one's deme or phratria, ἐς τὰ κοινὰ γραμματεῖα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.1; ἐγγράψαι τὸν υἱὸν εἰς ἄνδρας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.230; εἰς τοὺς φράτερας [prev. author] 39.4; ἐ. εἰς τοὺς ἀτίμους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 6; also ἱερὰν ἐ. τὴν οὐσίαν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 276}}:—_passive_, εἰς τοὺς δημότας ἐγγραφῆναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.261}}; Μαντίθεος ἐνεγεγράμμην by the name of M., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 39.4; τοὺς μήπω δι᾽ ἡλικίαν ἐγγεγραμμένους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1275a15; πρὶν ἐγγραφῆναι καὶ λαβεῖν τὸ χλαμύδιον 4th c.BC: Antidotus Comicus 2; εἰς τοὺς ἐφήβους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 366e. (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 699 is corrupt.)}}
**II.2** **indict**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1180, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.24:}} —_passive_, ἐγγράφεσθαι λιποταξίου **to be indicted** for desertion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.148.}}
**II.3** of statedebtors, **enter their names**, ἐγγραφόντων οἱ ἄρχοντες τοῖς πράκτορσιν Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.71}}; ἐγγεγραμμένος [ἐν ἀκροπόλει] **registered** among the state-debtors, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.4, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 48.1}}; also of ἄτιμοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 784d. (Perh. written ἐκγρ-, “SIG” 742.29.)}}

1450:
** ἔγγυος**, ον,
(ἐγγύη, compare ἀμφίγυος) **secured, under good security**, μνᾶς.. ἐγγύους ἐπὶτόκῳδεδανεις μένας [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.15 (but see at {ἔγγαιος} 11).}}]
**2** **reliable**, in _comparative_, ὅπλα -ώτερα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 15.197c (nisi to be read ἐχεγγ-).}}
**II** _substantive_, ={ἐγγυητής, ἔγγυον παρέχειν} {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 286, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.20, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1350a19, “SIG” 364.41 (from Ephesus), 976.13 (from Samos), “PEleph.” 8.19, NT.Heb.7.22, etc.}}; ἔ. τῆς προξενίας **giving security** for.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).4 (from Hypata), etc.: _feminine_ in 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator “Epistulae” 11.12, “BGU” 1051.10 (1st c.BC/ 1st c.AD).}}

1451:
** ἐγγύς** [ῠ],
_adverb_, _comparative_ ἐγγυτέρω (-τέρῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 6.3), also -ύτερον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 704e: _superlative_ ἐγγυτάτω}} or -ύτατα (first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus, and _Attic dialect_)}}; also ἔγγῑον, ἔγγιστα (see. ἐγγίων).
**I** of Place, **near, nigh, at hand**: frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἐ. γὰρ νυκτός τε καὶ ἤματός εἰσι κέλευθοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.86}}: with _genitive_, **hard by, near to**; so λύπας ἐγγυτέρω **nearer to** grief, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1217: with _dative_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.340, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 37}}; ἐγγὺς ὁδῷ uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.974: mostly with Verbs of rest, ἐ. ἑστάναι, παρεστάναι, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 686, “Eu.” 65; but ἐ. χωρεῖν [prev. author] “Th.” 59: with _genitive_, οἱ ἐγγυτάτω τῆς ἀγορᾶς κατεσκευασμένοι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.20, etc.}}
**II** of Time, **nigh at hand**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.453; ἐ. ἡμῖν ὁ ἀγών 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.2.}}
**III** of Numbers, etc., **nearly**, ἔτεσι ἐ. εἴκοσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.5; μισθὸς ἐ. ἐνιαυτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.28}}: generally, **nearly, almost**, ἐ. ἔγνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 301}}; οὐδ᾽ ἐ. τινος not **nearly**, i. e. not **by a great deal**, nothing **like** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198b}}; ἔχει οὐχ οὕτω ταῦτα οὐδ᾽ ἐ. not so.. nor yet **nearly so**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.30; οὐκ ἐποίουν τοῦτο, οὐδ᾽ ἐ. [prev. author] 18.96}}; **mostly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 34.}}
**IV** of Qualities, **coming near**, ἐ. τι καὶ παραπλήσιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 520a}}; ἐγγύτατα τοῦ νῦν τρόπου, τῆς ξυμπάσης γνώμης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13, 22; ὅτι ἐγγύτατα τούτων [prev. author] 7.86; κοινῇ δὲ πᾶσιν οὐδεὶς ἐγγυτέρω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.288; δοκεῖς δηλῶσαι ἐγγύτατα τὴν ῥητορικήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 452e}}; ἐ. εἶναι, with _genitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 116b; ἐ. τυφλῶν [prev. author] “R.” 508c}}; ἐ. τι τείνειν τοῦ τεθνάναι **very near** death, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 65a; κακῶς παθεῖν ἐγγύτατα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.123.}}
**V** of Relationship, **akin to**, οἱ Ζηνὸς ἐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 162}}; ἐγγυτέρω γένει or γένους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30a, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.72; ἐγγύτατα γένους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 388, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 41, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 304d; ἐγγυτάτω γένους “IG” 12.77, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1666.}}

1453:
** ἐγείρω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ _infinitive_ ἐγέρρην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 16.12, compare “Etymologicum Gudianum” 157.48}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἔγειρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.594}}: _future_ ἐγερῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epigrammata - PLG” 28 (compare ἐξ-, ἐπ-):}}_aorist_ ἤγειρα, _Epic dialect_ ἔγ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.44}}: _perfect_ ἐγήγερκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 16}}: _pluperfect_ -κειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.7.4, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.48}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330e, etc.}}: _future_ ἐγερθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 49.3 (also _future_ _middle_ ἐγεροῦμαι uncertain in 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 1.30.5)}}: _aorist_ ἠγέρθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.9, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔγερθεν variant for{ἄγ}. in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.287:}}_perfect_ ἐγήγερμαι variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.51}}: _pluperfect_ ἐγήγερτο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 19:}} also, in _passive_ sense, _poetry_ _aorist_ ἠγρόμην (ἐξ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 51}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔγρετο, _imperative_ ἔγρεο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.41, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.5}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ ἔγρῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 774; _optative_ ἔγροιτο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.113}}_infinitive_ ἐγρέσθαι (frequently written ἔγρεσθαι, as if from a _present_ ἔγρομαι, compare ἔγρω){{Refs|[prev. work] 13.124}}; _participle_ ἐγρόμενος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|10.50 (and late Prose, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.15)}}: _intransitive_ _perfect_ ἐγρήγορα (as _present_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 306, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310b}}, etc.: _pluperfect_ ἠγρηγόρη (as _imperfect_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 32}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐγρηγόρεσαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 744}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐγρηγόρει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.20}}: _Epic dialect_ _perfect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐγρηγόρθασι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.419; _imperative_ ἐγρήγορθε (see.below 11)}}_infinitive_ ἐγρήγορθαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 67.}}
**I** _active_, **awaken, rouse**, ἐ. τινὰ ἐξ ὕπνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5.413, etc.; τοὺς δ᾽.. ὑπνώοντας ἐγείρει 24.344; ἐ. τινὰ εὐνῆς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1050 \(Lyric poetry); simply, ἐ. τινά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 140, etc.: _metaphorically_, τὰς τέχνας 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.1.}}
**I.2** **rouse, stir up**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.208; ἐπεί μιν ἔγειρε Διὸς νόος 15.242}}; ἐγείρειν Ἄρηα **stir** the fight, {{Refs|2.440, etc.; ἐ. μάχην, φύλοπιν, etc., 13.778, 5.496}}, etc.; Τρωσὶν θυμὸν ἐ. (variant{ἀγεῖραι}){{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 510; ἐ. τινὰ ἐπὶ ἔργον 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 20; ἔγειρε νῆα “hymnus ad Apollinem” 408}}; ἐκδοχὴν πομποῦ πυρὸς ἐ. **wake up** the bale-fire, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 299}}; λαμπάδας ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 340: frequently _metaphorically_, ἐ. ἀοιδάν, λύραν, μέλος, θρῆνον, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.104, “N.” 10.21, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 222, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1778 (_anapaest meter_); μῦθον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 272d}}; τὸ οὖς ἐ. '**prick up**' the ears, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.1.12.}}
**I.3** **raise** from the dead, νεκρούς {{NT|NT.Matt.10.8, cf. NT.1Cor.15.42 (_passive_)}}; or from a sick-bed, {{NT|NT.Jam.5.15.}}
**I.4** **raise, erect** a building, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 103, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 64, “OGI” 677.3 (2nd c.AD)}}; ναόν {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.John.2.19, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 10}}:—_passive_, στῦλος ἐγηγερμένος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Bito Mechanicus 66.5, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 19, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 320c.}}
**II** _passive_, with _perfect_ _active_ ἐγρήγορα, **wake**, ἐγειρομένων ἀνθρώπων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.100, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.9, etc.; ἔγρετο δ᾽ ἐξ ὕπνου 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.41: _metaphorically_, ἐγειρόμενος εἰς ἐμαυτὸν ἐκ τοῦ σώματος 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.8.1}}: in _perfect_, **to be awake**, ἐγρηγόρθασι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.419}}; ἐγρήγορθε **stay awake** ! {{Refs|7.371, 18.299 (whereas ἔγρεο is}}**wake up**! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.46)}}; ἐγρήγορας ἢ καθεύδει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310b; πόλις ζῶσα καὶ ἐγρηγορυῖα [prev. author] “Lg.” 809d; καὶ ἐφρόνει καὶ ἐγρηγόρει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.20, etc.}}; of things, ἐγειρομένου χειμῶνος **arising**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.49: so _metaphorically_, τὰ ἐκ τοῦ βαρβάρου ἐγειρόμενα [prev. work] 148; ἐγρηγορὸς φρούρημα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 706; ἐ. τὸ πῆμα [prev. author] “Ag.” 346, etc.}}
**II.2** **rouse** or **stir oneself, be excited** by passion, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 176, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.305: with}}_infinitive_, ἐγηγερμένοι ἦσαν μὴ ἀνιέναι τὰ τῶν Ἀθηναίων they **were encouraged** to prevent the departure of the Athenians, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.51.}}
**III** _intransitive_ in _active_, **arouse oneself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 16b.}}
**IV** in ἀμφὶ πυρὴν.. ἔγρετο λαός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.434, 24.789, ἔγρ. is for ἤγρ-}} (ἀγείρω); so in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Maiistas Epicus 52.}}

1454:
** ἔγερσις**, εως, ἡ,
**awaking**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 82; personified in 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 123.1: _plural_, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.206S., 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 2.2.6: _metaphorically_, ἡ τοῦ θυμοῦ ἔ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 70c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1116b30.}}
**b** **awaking** from death, {{NT|NT.Matt.27.53}}; **recovery**, ἐκ τοῦ πάθεος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.11.}}
**2** **raising, erection**, τειχίων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.5.4 (_plural_), cf. Menander Ephesius Historicus cited in 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.5.3.}}

1455:
** ἐγκάθ-ετος**, ον,
(ἐγκαθίημι) **put in secretly, suborned**, προέδρους ἐ. ὑφέντες {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 368e, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.34, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.5.1, NT.Luke.20.20, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.5.2}}. _adverb_ -τως, δημηγορεῖν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.68.}}
**II** of a child, ={εἰσποιητός}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 56.}}

1457:
** ἐγκαιν-ίζω**,
**restore**, τεῖχος {{LXX|LXX.Isa.16.11; βασιλείαν LXX.1Ki.11.14}}; **make afresh** ὁδόν {{NT|NT.Heb.10.20}}; **consecrate, inaugurate**, οἶκον Κυρίου {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.8.63}}:—_passive_, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Heb.9.18, “IG” 12(5).712.58 (from Syros); χύτρα -ισμένα 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.46.4.}}
**II** **innovate**, probably in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 16.24 (2nd c.BC).}}

1458:
** ἐγκᾰλέω**,
**call in** a debt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.33, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 31.6, 36.14}}: generally, **demand** as one's due, ἀργύριον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.26.}}
**2** **invoke**, τὴν τῶν θεῶν ἰατρείαν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.1.44.}}
**II** **bring a charge** or **accusation against** a person:—Constr.: with _dative_ _person_ et _accusative_ _of things_, **charge** something **against** one, φόνους ἐ. τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 778, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26c, etc.; ἐ. ἔγκλημά τινι 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 18, compare “Eux.” 24}}; χόλον κατ᾽ αὐτῶν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 328: followed by a _relative_ clause, ἐ. τινὶ ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.5.7}}: with _infinitive_, ἐστὶν ἃ ἐνεκάλει τοῖς Ἀθηναίοις παραβαίνειν τὰς σπονδάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.123}}: with _participle_, ἐ. αὐτοῖς ἀμελοῦσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 346a}}: frequently with _dative_ _person_ only, **accuse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.2, etc.; ἐ. περί τινων “Inscription Prien.” 28.8 (2nd c.BC); ἐπὶ τοῖς διῳκημένοις [prev. work]37.128 (2nd c.BC)}}: with _accusative_ _of things_ only, **bring as a charge**, εἴ τι ἄλλο ἐνεκάλουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.46, compare 6.53}}; τὸ νεῖκος ἐγκαλεῖν **throw the blame** of quarrel on another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 702: _absolutely_, οἱ ἐγκαλέσαντες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rh.Al.” 1437a17: rarely with _genitive_ _of things_, τῆς βραδύτητος αὐτοῖς ἐνεκάλει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 10:}} —_passive_, ἐγκαλεῖται τῇ τύχῃ **a charge is brought against**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1120b17; ἐνίων ἐγκληθέντων ἐπὶ τῷ βίῳ 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” p.93 G.}}; τὰ ἐγκεκλημένα **charges**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.14 (2nd c.BC)}}: also with person. _construction_, ἐγκαλεῖσθαι ὑπέρ τινος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.46; τινός 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 58.4:}} — _Locrian dialect_ _participle_ ἐγκαλείμενος may be either _middle_ or _passive_, {{Refs|“IG” 9(1).334.41.}}
**II.2** as lawterm, **prosecute, take proceedings against**, οὔτ᾽ ἐγκαλοῦντες οὔτ᾽ ἐγκαλούμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.1; ἐ. δίκην τινί [prev. author] 40.19; ἐ. τινὶ περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.40: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1455.}}
**II.3** **object**, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 29.}}

1459:
** ἐγκατα-λείπω**,
**leave behind**, παῖδα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 378; ἐ. φρουρὰν ἐν τῇ νήσῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.51; πλεῖον ἐ. ἐξιόντες ἐκ τῆς ὠνῆς “PRev.Laws” 53.12}}; ἐ. τὸ κέντρον, of a bee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91c}}: hence of Pericles, τὸ κέντρον ἐγκατέλιπε τοῖς ἀκροωμένοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 94.7; ἐ. τὴν μάχαιραν ἐν τῇ σφαγῇ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.69.}}
**2** **leave in the lurch**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 179a, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 2, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.58, NT.Matt.27.46, “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 4.22, etc.}}; **abandon**, νεκροὺς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.44; ἀκρόπολιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.13.}}
**3** **leave out, omit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.119.}}
**4** **leave traces behind**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Nat.Herc.” 1420.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be left behind** in a race, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.59.}}
**II.2** **leave residual symptoms** or **sequelae**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.2.6, 6.7.7.}}

1461:
** ἐγκεντ-ρίζω**,
**goad, spur on**, {{LXX|LXX.Wis.16.11 (_passive_).}}
**II** of plants, **graft**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.2.5, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Gaurum - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 10.1 (_passive_)}}, etc.: _metaphorically_, {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Rom.11.17, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.7}}; of a Centaur, ὃν φύσις ἐνεκέντρισεν ἵππῳ {{Refs|“APl.” 4.116 (from Euodus).}}
**III** (κέντρον) **concentrate**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 74:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 263.}}

1462:
** ἔγ-κλημα**, ατος, τό,
(ἐγκαλέω) **accusation, charge**, ἔ. τινι ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 323, compare “Tr.” 361, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.9, etc.}}; ἐγκλήματα ἔχειν τινός, ={ἐγκαλεῖν τι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26}}; ἔ. ποιεῖν τι make a thing **matter of complaint**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.53}}; ἐγκλήματα ποιεῖσθαι bring **accusations**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.126}}; τὰ ἐ. τὰ ἔς τινας **complaints** respecting..,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 79; ἐν ἐγκλήματι γίγνεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.251}}; γίγνεται or ἐστὶ ἔγκλημά μοι πρός τινα I have **ground of complaint** respecting him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.6, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.23}}; λύειν ἔ. **clear away** a charge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.52.4; λόγοις τὰ ἐ. διαλύεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140.}}
**II** in Law, **written complaint**: generally, of **complaints** which were to lead to private suits, ἔ. λαγχάνειν τινί file **a complaint** against.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.16, +others, compare “PTeb.” 616 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** concrete, **a standing reproach**, τῆς τύχης καὶ τῶν θεῶν {{Refs|Plu “Dio” 58.}}
**III.2** **defect**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.20.}}

1463:
** ἐγκομβ-όομαι**,
_middle_, (κόμβος) **bind** a thing **on oneself, wear** it **constantly**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Carystius Comicus 4, NT.1Pet.5.5.}}
**II** _passive_, ={δέομαι},{ἐνειλοῦμαι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 7.}}

1464:
** ἐγκοπ-ή**, ἡ,
**incision**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.38}}; **fracture** of skull, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de signis fracturarum - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 3, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 46.12.1.}}
**2** **steps** cut in the wall of wells, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 90.}}
**II** **hindrance**, οἴησις προκοπῆς ἐ. {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 131, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.6, NT.1Cor.9.12, Vett. Val. 2.7 (_plural_)}}; material **obstacle**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.32}}; **interruption, check**, τῆς ἁρμονίας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22; τοῦ λόγου 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “τέχναι ῥητορικαί” 2p.514S., compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21}}; κατ᾽ ἐγκοπάς **disjointedly**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 41.3.}}

1465:
** ἐγκόπτω**,
**knock in**, πάτταλον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.7.6}}; χιλίας (i.e. πληγὰς) ἐς τὸ νῶτον ἐγκόψαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.33.}}
**2** **engrave**, τὸ ψήφισμα ἐς στήλην {{Refs|“SIG” 279.33 (from Zelea)}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).536 (from Thera).}}
**3** **incise**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.141 (_passive_).}}
**II** **oppose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 13; λόγον λόγῳ 6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 125.9.}}
**III** **check, hold** the breath, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.69}}: generally, **hinder, thwart**, τισί {{Refs|“Sammelb.” 4305}}; τῇ δικαιοδοσίᾳ _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.1.12}}; **delay,** {{NT|NT.Acts.24.4}}:—_passive_, ἐνεκοπτόμην τοῦ ἐλθεῖν {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Rom.15.22, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 19.}}
**IV** _intransitive_, **come to a stop**, {{Refs|Vett. Val. 260.24.}}

1466:
** ἐγκράτ-εια** [ρᾰ], ἡ,
**mastery over**, ἐ. ἑαυτοῦ self-**control**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 390b}}; ἐ. ἡδονῶν καὶ ἐπιθυμιῶν **control over** them,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 430e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.21; περί τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1149a21, +others}}
**II** _absolutely_, **self-control**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.44, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1145b8, +others, LXX.Sir.18.30, NT.Act.24.25, etc.}}

1467:
** ἐγκρατ-εύομαι**,
**exercise self-control**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1223b12, LXX.Gen.43.31, NT.1Cor.7.9}}; **force oneself** to do a thing, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.13.12}}; **starve oneself**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 127.20 (compare ἀποκαρτερεῖν).}}
**II** **to be a member of the Encratite sect, Anatolian Studies** {{Refs|87 (from Phrygia).}}

1468:
** ἐγκρατ-ής**, ές,
(κράτος) **in possession of power**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 941.}}
**II** **holding fast**, χεὶρ -εστάτη a hand **with the firmest hold**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.8.}}
**II.2** **stout, strong**, ἐ. σθένει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 55; τὸν -έστατον σίδηρον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 474; ἐ. σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.23.}}
**III** with _genitive_ _of things_, **having possession of**, χωρέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.49, compare 9.106, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 75, “SIG” 58.7 (Milet., 5th c.BC), etc.}}; ναὸς ἐγκρατῆ πόδα the sheet **that controls** the ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 715}}; ἐ. αὑτῶν **masters of** themselves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 256b, +others; ἐ. ἀφροδισίων καὶ γαστρός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.1, compare 2.1.7, “Oec.” 12.16.}}
**III.2** _absolutely_, **master of oneself, self-controlled**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 415d}}; **self-disciplined**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1145b13, +others}}
**IV** _adverb_ -τῶς, _Ionic dialect_ - τέως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐγκατατομῆς ἐμβρύοι” 4, etc.}}; **with a strong hand, by force**, ἄρχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76; ἐ. ἔχειν τὴν ἀρχήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284a40}}; -τέως **forcibly**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.266.}}
**IV.2** **with self-control, temperately**, ἐ. ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 710a; φέρειν τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 2.53; - τέως 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 23.20.}}

1469:
** ἐγκρίνω** [ῑ],
**reckon in** or **among: reckon as**, τίν᾽ ἄνδρ᾽ ἄριστον ἐγκρίναιεν ἄν; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 183.}}
**2** of persons, **select, admit**, ἐ. ἢ συγκρ. {{NT|NT.2Cor.10.12}}:—_passive_, εἰς τὴν αἵρεσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 755d; εἰς τὴν γερουσίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.107; [εἰς] τὸ στάδιον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.40}}; of ἔφηβοι, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 7.29 (3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; of athletes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.59}}; ἐγκρινόμενος, ὁ, subject of statue by Alcamenes, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 34.72.}}
**3** **admit, accept**, opposed to ἀποκρίνω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936a; ἐν τοῖς φιλοσόφοις [prev. author] “R.” 486d, compare “Lg.” 952a, +others; τρία γένη σημείων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 32}}; παλιγγενεσίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.104}}; **regard as genuine**, νομίσματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.256 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; **admit, sanction**, e.g. an author as classical, [{{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {Δείναρχος}.}}]
**4** **approue**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.2.4 (_passive_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.11f}}; ἀριθμὸς ἐγκρίνεται **is adopted**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 8.3.}}

1470:
** ἐγ-κρύπτω**,
**hide** or **conceal in**, δαλὸν σποδιῇ ἐνέκρυψε μελαίνῃ {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.488, compare 4th c.BC: Sotades Comicus 1.29; [ᾠὰ] ἐν δέρματι λαγωοῦ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 619b15; τι εἴς τι NT.Matt.13.33, 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 1.5.1 (_passive_), etc.}}
**2** πῦρ ἐ. **bank** it **up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 841.}}
**3** _middle_, **hide oneself**, μελάθροις {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 32.285.}}

1471:
** ἔγκῠος**, ον,
(κύω) ={ἐγκύμων}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5, 6.131, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.42, etc.}}; πῶλος ἡσυχίης ἔγκυος, of the Trojan horse, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.156 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus); γαστρὸς ἀπωσαμέναν μόρον ἔγκυον, of one dying in child-birth, “Epigr Gr.” 238 (from Smyrna),compare “IG” 12(7).301 (from Amorgos).}}
**2** of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 595b27.}}

1472:
** ἐγχρίω** [ῑ],
**anoint**, ἀλείμμασιν ἑαυτόν {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Duris Epigrammaticus 10 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 11.117 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus); τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς NT.Rev.3.18: _metaphorically_, ψευδηγόροις φήμαις ἐγχρίειν ἔπη 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1455}}:—_middle_, **anoint oneself**, ἰξοῦ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.29}}; ἐ. τὸ πρόσωπον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Nic.Dam. p.2 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator: _absolutely_, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.21.20, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.526.}}
**II** **sting, prick**, τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251d:}} —_passive_, ἰὸς ἐγχρισθείς poison **injected by a sting**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 1.54.}}
**II.2** **stick in**, τὸ κέντρον {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.20.}}

1473:
** ἐγώ**,
I: _pronoun_ of the first person:—_Epic dialect_ mostly **ἐγών** before vowels (so in _Doric dialect_, before consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 85, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748, 754), rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱών** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.4}}:— strengthened **ἔγωγε**, **I at least, for my part, indeed, for myself** (more frequently in _Attic dialect_ than in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἐγώνγα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, “Lys.” 986, _dative_ ἐμίνγα “IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph.)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱώνγα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21}}; **ἱώνει** {{Refs|Ead. 10}}; **ἰώγα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 898}}: _Laconian dialect_ and _Tarentum dialect_ **ἐγώνη**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.29.}}
**II** oblique cases from a different root, _genitive_ ἐμοῦ, _enclitic_ μο; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἐμέο, ἐμεῦ, μευ, also ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμεθεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7; ἐμεῖο “IG” 3.1337; μεθέν 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 20}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμέος, ἐμεῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 144}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐμοῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 37; also ἐμῶς, ἐμίο, ἐμίω, ἐμίως 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.17:}} — _dative_ ἐμοί, _enclitic_ μοι (which may be compared with Sanskrit _genitive_ **me** in κλῦθί μοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.115, +others); ὅ μοι πόσις “Schwyzer” 683 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99, “AJA” 29.461 (Rhodian, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 733, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.30}}; _Tarentum dialect_ ἐμίνη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13: _accusative_ ἐμέ}}, _enclitic_ μ; {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria μι “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59,60 H.}}
**III** dual, _nominative_ and _accusative_, **νῶι**, **we two**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.34, etc.}}; _accusative_ νῶιν Zenod.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.377}}; _Attic dialect_ νώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475); νῶε 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 39, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 5: _genitive_, _dative_ νῶι; νῷν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; νῶι _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 773}}; νῶιν, ={ἡμῖν}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.213, etc.}}
**IV** _plural_, _nominative_ ἡμεῖς (ἡμέες _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6, +others, rejected by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.1)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.303, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμές {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 65, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168:— _genitive_ ἡμῶν (also ἥμων 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.23)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.; ἡμείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.170, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.46}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88, “Milet.” 3 No.152.29}}; ἄμμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]74, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 66}}; ἁμῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.158}}; Cretan dialect, _Boeotian dialect_ ἁμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 528.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.21:— _dative_ ἡμῖν, in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus also ἡμίν}} (ῐ) (or ἧμιν Aristarch.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3)}}; also rarely in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386 (uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμῐν, ἄμμῐ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 156 (Lyric poetry), “Milet.” 3 No.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

1474:
** ἐδᾰφ-ίζω**,
**beat level and firm like a floor** or **pavement**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.33.6, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.3.1}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “CP” 4.8.2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 934b10.}}
**II** **provide with a floor**, οἶκον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(2).158 A 66 (3rd c.BC),compare “BCH” 29.475.}}
**III** **dash to the ground**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.136(137).9, NT.Luke.19.44.}}

1475:
** ἔδᾰφος**, εος, τό,
**bottom, foundation, base** of anything, τῆς κατασκευῆς τὰ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; ἔ. νηός **bottom** of a ship, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.249; ἔ. πλοίου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.5, compare 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 12}}; ἔ. ποταμοῦ, τῆς θαλάττης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.18, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 534a11}}; [ποτηρίου] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 143.2.}}
**2** **ground-floor, pavement**, οἴκου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.137}}; καθελεῖν ἐς ἔ. raze to the **ground**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68; τὸ ἔ. ὁμαλίσασι “IG” 11(2).161 A 57 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἔπεσον εἰς τὸ ἔ. {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Act.22.7; ἀπὸ ἐδάφους μέχρι παντὸς ὕψους “CPR” 95.17 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** **ground, soil**, περὶ τοῦ τῆς πατρίδος ἐδάφους ἀγωνίζεσθαι for our country's **soil**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.134, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.11 (_plural_)}}; ἐχθρὸς τῷ τῆς πόλεως ἐδάφει, of a mortal foe, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.39, 10.11; ὀκρυόειν ἔ. “Eleg.Alex. Adesp.” 1.7}}; **soil**, viewed in regard to its quality, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.4.1 (_plural_), 4.11.8: _plural_, ἐδάφη}} **lands and tenements** (_including_ houses), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.42, “IG” 2.780, “PTeb.” 302.10 (1st c.AD)}}; also, **masses of earth**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.48U.}}
**4** **text** of a manuscript, opposed to margin (μέτωπον), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.837, 18(2).864.}}
**4.b** **manuscript**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 16.468 (assuming variant).}}
**5** **background** of puppet-theatre, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 30.1, +others}}

1476:
** ἑδραῖος**, α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407b, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.288d}}:— **sitting, sedentary**, of persons or their occupations, ἔργον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 53; οἱ πολλοὶ τῶν τὰς τέχνας ἐχόντων ἑδραῖοί εἰσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.3}}; ἑ. ἀρχαί, opposed to στρατεῖαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407b; ἑ. βίος “Anthologia Graeca” 11.42 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** ἑδραία ῥάχις the horse's back **on which the rider sits**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 783.}}
**II** **steady, steadfast**, κάθησ᾽ ἑδραία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Andr.” 266; δεῖ τὴν γυναῖκα ὥσπερ κύβον ἑδραῖον εἶναι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.288d, compare952d; κύβος -ότατον σῶμα 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 98c; ἑ. βάσεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 59d; ἑδραιότατον στοιχεῖον εἶναι τὴν γῆν 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus “Allegoriae Quaestiones Homericae” 41; ὂν τὸ πάντων -ότατον 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.8}}; ἑ. ὕπνος **sound** sleep, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.4.15}}; of a cup, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.496a}}: _metaphorically_ in Rhet., **firmly based**, κατάληξις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 19, compare 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 40.4}}. _adverb_ -αίως **firmly**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 36.10, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.14.5}}; **steadily**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 3.21.}}
**II.2** **permanently appointed,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PStrassb. 40.11 (6th c.AD).}}

1480:
** ἐθ-ίζω**,
_poetry_ **εἰθ-**{{Refs|“Carmen Aureum” 35}}: _Attic dialect_ _future_ ἐθιῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.53}}: _aorist_ εἴθισα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.68}}: _perfect_ εἴθικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 70b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.15}}:—_passive_, _future_ ἐθισθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.11}}: _aorist_ εἰθίσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 512, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 41, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681b}}: _perfect_ εἴθισμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 122 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἰθίδαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 36; late ἤθισμαι “IG” 12(5).662.14 (Syros, 2nd c.AD)}}: _pluperfect_ εἴθιστο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.2}}: (ἔθος):—**accustom**, ἐ. αὑτὸν χαίρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 510d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.57; τὸ προαιρεῖσθαι.. πότερον ἂν ἐθίζοιμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.2}}: with _infinitive_, ἐθίσας ἀεί τι λῄζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἀννιβαϊκή” 44: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἔθη ἐ. πονηρά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 706d; ἐ. τινὰ ταὐτά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.15; ἐ. τινὰ πρός τι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 20}}:—_passive_, **to be** or **become accustomed** or **used to** do, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 41, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 512, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.31, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77, etc.; εἰθισμένος ἀναισχυντεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.4: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἐθίζεσθαι ἔθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681b; ἐθίζεσθαι σὺν ἔθει τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.33 (assuming variant); ἐθίζεσθαι πρός τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1119a25}}; τι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1121a23; τινί 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.9.11: _absolutely_, καθότι εἴθισται}} as **is the custom,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3p.116 (3rd c.BC)}}; κατὰ τὰ εἰθις μένα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 1073.12 (3rd c.AD)}}, etc.:—in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 12, Bekk. restored εἰθίζοντο from 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus for the _intransitive_}}_active_ εἴθιζον.
**II** _intransitive_ in _active_, **become accustomed**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 10.22}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 12.2: with _accusative_, ἔθιζε καὶ ὅσα ἀπογινώσκεις [prev. work] 6: with}}_infinitive_ supplied, ὅπως ἀναγραφῇ τὸ ψήφισμα οὗ καὶ τὰ ἄλλα ἐθίζουσιν (i.e. ἀναγράψαι) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 48.370(Thaumaci, 1st c.BC).}}

1481:
** ἐθν-άρχης**, ου, ὁ,
**ruler of a tribe** or **nation**, Ἄσανδρος ἀντὶ ἐθνάρχου βασιλεὺς ἀναγορευθεὶς βοσπόρου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 17}}; **sheikh**, {{Refs|“OGI” 616.2 (from Arabia)}}; of Abraham, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.513.}}
**2** title of Jewish official, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|LXX.1Mac.14.47, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.13, Nic.Dam. p.143 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, NT.2Cor.11.32, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 13.6.7.}}
**II** _adjective_, **ruling over nations**, ἐ. θεοί {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “contra Galilaeos” 115d, compare 143a.}}

1482:
** ἐθν-ικός**, ή, όν,
**national**, συστάσεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.13.6; διαστάσεις [prev. author] 4.21.2; χρεῖαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.13; ἰδιότητες 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.154 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; διαφοραί 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.3.1.}}
**II** **foreign, gentile,** {{NT|NT.Matt.5.47; ἐθνικῇ.. ἐν σοφία “epigram Gr.” 430.6.}} _adverb_ -κῶς, opposed to Ἰουδαϊκῶς{{NT|NT.Gal.2.14.}}
**II.b** in the Roman Empire, **provincial, Codex Just.**{{Refs|12.63.2.6.}}
**III** Grammars, **indicating nationality**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.28, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 636.11, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 190.20.}} _adverb_ -κῶς, παραχθέν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 5, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 4.1.1, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.56.}}
**III.2** **dialectal**, ἔθος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 46.1.}}
**IV** ἐθνικός, ὁ, **tax-collector,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 126.13 (6th c.AD).}}

